       <details class="dfs-method"><summary>How projections and lineup builds work</summary><p>GOING begins with its injury-adjusted player distribution. For yardage, receptions and passing touchdowns, a fresh sportsbook consensus can move the mean modestly: 35% market with at least two distinct books, 20% with one, otherwise the GOING model stands alone. Extreme alternate lines are excluded by requiring a normally priced two-sided market. Touchdowns remain model-based because an anytime-TD price is a chance of one or more scores, not expected touchdown count.</p><p>DraftKings scoring uses full PPR and a $50,000 cap; FanDuel uses half PPR and a $60,000 cap. Both builds include expected yardage bonuses. Single-game mode uses six players from one game and ranks the Captain/MVP using the player's ceiling estimate; the multiplier slot earns 1.5× points and, with an official salary file, costs 1.5× salary. Every roster includes both teams. Ownership is unavailable and is never invented.</p><p>GOING currently lacks interception, fumble and ownership projections, so those components are explicitly absent. Projection-only mode ranks GOING's current slate without salaries; salary-file mode adds the cap plus platform D/ST/kicker rows. D/ST and kickers use imported platform FPPG until GOING has validated specialty projections. Balanced maximizes projected points. Floor discounts uncertainty. Ceiling rewards upside and QB–receiver correlation. Suggested lineups are research outputs, not automatic entries.</p></details>
